hypotheses.statement:
On every observation face retained in the graph insert $$\label{prep:cutoff-definition}
 \chi_T(d_f)=\int_{-T}^{T}\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}
                   e^{-(d_f-x)^2/2}\,dx,
 \qquad d_f=y_{c'}-y_c+l_f,\qquad T=\sqrt{s}.$$

evidence.quote:
For $n_j=L^j/2$, there exists $\epsilon>0$ such that $$\log a_{n_{j+1}}-\log a_{n_j}
   =-\frac{\log L}{8}+\frac1{16j}
       +O_L(j^{-1-\epsilon}).
 \label{eq:terminal-ratio}$$

outputs.exact_statement:
Using the height, renormalization, and spin-field inputs from (OpenAI 2026a, 2026b, 2026c) [[B](#ref-B), [X](#ref-X), [S](#ref-S)] stated in Section <a href="#sec:inputs" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="sec:inputs">2</a>, there is a constant $A_{\mathrm{XY}}\in(0,\infty)$ such that $$\label{eq:main}
 a_n=A_{\mathrm{XY}}\,n^{-1/8}(\log n)^{1/16}(1+o(1))
                       \qquad(n\to\infty).$$

For the mass threshold $b_c$ in Theorem <a href="#thm:main" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="thm:main">1</a>, $0<b_c<\infty$, $m(b_c)=0$, and the primary height coefficient of (OpenAI 2026b) [[X](#ref-X)] satisfies $$\label{inp:critical-coefficient}
                              a(b_c)=8\pi.$$ At a general positive coefficient $a=a(b)>0$, the following statements hold.*

1.  *On free rectangles tending after scaling to a fixed nondegenerate rectangle $U$, the tests $\langle h,A_{U_N}f(\cdot/N)\rangle$, for smooth $f$ near $\overline U$, have joint Gaussian Laplace and moment limits with covariance $a\int_U\nabla f\cdot\nabla g$, where the lattice mesh is $N^{-1}$. Mean-free density smears have covariance $a(-\Delta_{U,N})^{-1}$; bounded piecewise continuous densities and face smears, centered by a volume smear, are allowed.*

2.  *In a fixed polygonal geometry made of finitely many rectangular pieces, with positive-width passages and box pin regions $p$, the corresponding limits have precision $$a^{-1}\int_U|\nabla v|^2,
     \qquad v\in H^1(U),\quad v=0\ \hbox{on }p.$$ Free components are taken modulo constants. A fixed finite-rank nonnegative quadratic penalty in box averages gives the corresponding Gaussian penalty; if it controls all free constants, those constants are integrated in the limit. Bounded-step coherent rounding is permitted.*

3.  *The fractional free mean modulo $2\pi$ tends to uniform measure, independently of the Gaussian observations. If $u_m$ is the uniform probability on an $m$-square and $t_m$ is a zero-total profile of bounded reference cost, then for every fixed nonzero integer $k$, $$\mathbb Ee^{i\langle h,ku_m+t_m\rangle}\longrightarrow0.$$ Writing $\bar\rho_m$ for the average of the four face probabilities and $z_m=\mathbb Ee^{i\langle h,\bar\rho_m\rangle}$, one has $$\label{inp:face-ratio}
     \liminf_{m\to\infty}\frac{z_{Gm}}{z_m}
           \ge c_bG^{2-a/(4\pi)}$$ for sufficiently large fixed integers $G$, with $c_b>0$ independent of $G$.

Let $r$ be the square minimizer at side $M$ and $r_\infty$ the plane height just defined. If an axis rectangle and its fixed collar avoid the source, choose an integral gauge potential $g$ there with $\partial g=l$. The local real lift is $\lambda=(r+g)/\alpha$, and $\partial\lambda=\eta_l/\alpha$. At interior supports meeting the source, the translation from the pierced-plane calculation to the square is $$\label{eq:lift-difference}
                         \gamma_M=(r-r_\infty)/\alpha.$$ In lattice units, for the fixed derivative orders used in the maps, $$|\nabla^a\eta_l(x)|\le C_a(1+\operatorname{dist}(x,\text{source}))^{-1-a}.$$ In an inner fixed fraction of the square, $$\label{eq:lift-derivatives}
                 |\nabla^a\gamma_M|\le C_aM^{-a},
                 \qquad 1\le a\le5.$$ The connection estimate includes the reflected stencils at free walls. The affine contribution $(B_sr)_{c'}-(B_sr)_c+l_f$ to every retained observation difference is bounded uniformly in $s$ and $M$.

For every preparation graph $G$ and every real observation vector $y$, $$\label{eq:large-observation}
 \frac{Z_G^l(y)}{Z_G^0(0)}
       \le\exp\left\{-c\sum_{\substack{f\in\mathcal F(G)\\|d_f|\le2T}}
                                                    d_f^2\right\}.$$ Consequently, with a possibly smaller constant $c>0$ independent of $R,P,s$, $$\label{prep:face-saving}
 \frac{Z_G^l(y)}{Z_G^0(0)}
             \prod_{f\in\mathcal F(G)}\chi_T(d_f)
                  \le e^{-c\sum_{f\in\mathcal F(G)}d_f^2}.$$

On each open preparation graph there are a quadratic form $L_G$ with a fixed-radius stencil in the faces, and a symmetric matrix $N_G$ on those faces, such that $$\label{prep:shape-decomposition}
 E_G^l(y)=L_G(d)+\sum_{f,g\in\mathcal F(G)}N_G(f,g)d_fd_g,$$ where $$\label{prep:trial-energy}
 0\le L_G(d)\le C\sum_fd_f^2
                       +C\log(2s)\sum_z\kappa_z(d)^2.$$ The forms and matrices may be defined on arbitrary face arrays $d$, without imposing a curl constraint. Their nonlocal entries satisfy $$\label{prep:matrix-decay}
                         |N_G(f,g)|\le Ce^{-c\operatorname{dist}(f,g)}.$$ Their dependence on the graph is exponentially localized as well. In particular, if faces in a set $D$ are deleted, extend both matrices by zero on missing faces. With a fixed stencil enlargement understood in the distances, $$\label{prep:matrix-cut}
 |(N_G-N_{G\setminus D})(f,g)|
 \le C\exp\left\{-c\left(\operatorname{dist}(f,g)
       +\min\{\operatorname{dist}(f,D),\operatorname{dist}(g,D)\}\right)
                                                   \right\}.$$ The local stencils agree wherever the graphs agree. For the actual one-source data this implies $$\label{eq:affine-shape-bound}
 E_G^l(y)\le C\sum_{f\in\mathcal F(G)}d_f^2
                  +C\log(2s)\mathbf 1_{\{\text{pierced star present}\}}.$$

Choose the regulator reserves as in <a href="#eq:entry-admissible-reserve" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:entry-admissible-reserve">[eq:entry-admissible-reserve]</a>, and fix compatible parameters for the local maps of (OpenAI 2026a) [[B](#ref-B)], including the block factor $L$, the support geometry, the activity weight $\mathfrak A$, and the analytic radius. A fixed finite collection of stronger weights and entry-admissible regulator regimes may be specified. For every $\epsilon>0$, one can choose a fixed dyadic $q$, an arbitrarily large dyadic $R$, and then $s_0$, such that the following conclusions hold for every compatible dyadic $s\ge s_0$, with $P=R^5$ and $m=qRs$.*


1.  *In the ordinary case, averaging the observation noise and the reference covariance below $m$ gives local activities $K_m$ satisfying $$\label{eq:entry-small}
                     \lVert K_m\rVert_{m,\mathfrak A,V}\le\epsilon.$$ This holds on finite plane supports, on compatible tori, and on free squares whose sides are divisible by $sP$ and are at least $sP$. Up to a positive field-independent scalar, the corresponding subset partition function is exactly the averaged cutoff interaction. Activities with their required clearance from a wall or a torus identification are the corresponding plane activities. The construction is real, even, and covariant under the block translations and square symmetries that preserve the chosen hierarchy.*



2.  *In a pierced free square in which the compulsory footprint and its required collars fit, the same scalar as in its ordinary counterpart may be extracted. The remaining partition function is $$\label{eq:entry-linear-partition}
                        \mathcal Z_m^{(1)}(K_m^l,I_m;\psi),$$ with exactly one insertion activity $I_m$. Its supports contain the mark block and the compulsory cover just specified. The background activities $K_m^l$ are arbitrarily small in their prescribed norms, and $$\label{eq:entry-insertion}
                        \lVert I_m\rVert_{m,\mathfrak A,V}
                             \le C_R s^{C_R}.$$ Here and below $C_R$ may also depend on the fixed downstream norm and geometric parameters, but not on $s$ or on the square side. Ordinary occupied supports remain separated from the insertion by the original compatibility rule.*

3.  *A support is called *shift-good* if its spanning rectangle, enlarged by $Hm$, misses the magnetic mark. The fixed constant $H$ is chosen from the local-map geometry. On such supports the pierced background is the ordinary background evaluated at the local lift $\psi+\lambda$, where $$\partial\lambda=\eta_l/\alpha.$$ On interior supports near the mark, including insertion supports, the activities are copies of the pierced-plane calculation evaluated at $\psi+\gamma_M$, with $\gamma_M$ and its derivative bounds as in <a href="#eq:lift-difference" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:lift-difference">[eq:lift-difference]</a> and <a href="#eq:lift-derivatives" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:lift-derivatives">[eq:lift-derivatives]</a>. Copying always includes the collars and numerical dependence required by the activity.*

*The assertions hold simultaneously in the fixed finite collection of reserve regimes. Their constants are uniform after the geometric parameters have been fixed.

As $N\to\infty$ with $s/N\to0$, locally uniformly for complex $w$, $$\begin{aligned}
 \mathbb Ee^{wX_N}&\longrightarrow e^{agw^2/2},
 \label{eq:bulk-physical-gradient}\\
 \mathbb E(\chi_Ne^{wX_N})-\mathbb E\chi_N\,e^{agw^2/2}
 &\longrightarrow0.
 \label{eq:bulk-physical-phase}
\end{aligned}$$ All fixed source derivatives converge as well. For $s/N\le P^{-1}$, the discrepancies can be made uniformly small by first increasing $R$, hence $P$, and then increasing $s$. The same statements hold with the cutoff on every stopping torus of a $J$-history, uniformly up to index $8J$.

For every sufficiently small prescribed tube radius, the entry parameters can be chosen so that all $J$-histories, for sufficiently large $J$, remain in that tube through index $8J$. The stopped torus remainders remain in a fixed comparable tube. Moreover, for every fixed $0<c<8$, $$\label{eq:bulk-window-vanishing}
 \sup_{cJ\le k\le8J}
 \bigl(|u_k^{(J)}|+|Z_k^{(J)}|
       +\lVert B_{e,k}^{(J)}\rVert+\lVert B_{o,k}^{(J)}\rVert\bigr)
 \longrightarrow0.$$ The analogous stopped torus remainder quantities tend to zero.

For every $0<c<8$, uniformly on $cJ\le k\le8J$, $$\begin{aligned}
 U_k&=\frac{a}{1-u_k^{(J)}}+O((Z_k^{(J)})^2)+O(e^{-c'J}),
 \label{eq:bulk-U-readout}\\
 Y_k&=M_kZ_k^{(J)}
 +O\bigl((|u_k^{(J)}|+(Z_k^{(J)})^2)Z_k^{(J)}\bigr)
 +O(e^{-c'J}).
 \label{eq:bulk-Y-readout}
\end{aligned}$$ Here $c'>0$ may depend on $c$, and $M_k$ is bounded above and below by positive constants and is common to all histories, up to an exponentially small error which is included in the display. The coordinate changes also give $$\label{eq:bulk-coordinate-conversion}
 t_k=u_k+O(u_k^2+Z_k^2),\qquad
 z_k=\ell_k^{-1}Z_k+O((|u_k|+Z_k^2)Z_k),\qquad
 \lVert\mathcal R_k\rVert\le C(u_k^2+Z_k^2),$$ where $\ell_k$ is the absolute-index fundamental balancing factor and is bounded above and below positively.

For all sufficiently large $j$, the exact maps from entry scale $i$ to stop $h$ can be chosen with the following properties.*


1.  *At the stop, in the fixed norm regimes for the ordinary and defect activities, $$\label{eq:mag-small-backgrounds}
     \max_{Q\in\{M,LM\}}
     \bigl(\lVert K^0_{h,Q}\rVert+\lVert K^l_{h,Q}\rVert\bigr)\le \frac{C}{j}.$$ The bulk copies satisfy the sharper conclusion of Proposition <a href="#prop:bulk-window" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:bulk-window">21</a>. On shift-good supports, pierced activities are exactly ordinary activities evaluated at the local lift. Interior pierced activities copy the plane calculation at the translate $\gamma_Q$, whenever their required clearance holds.*



2.  *Let $a^\nu_{k,Q,b}$ be the designated scalar coefficient at coarse block $b$ in step $k$, for $\nu=0,l$, and set $$\label{eq:mag-background-products}
     P^\nu_Q=\prod_{k=i}^{h-1}\prod_b(1+a^\nu_{k,Q,b}).$$ All factors are real and positive. The ratios agree exactly: $$\label{eq:mag-common-background-ratio}
     \frac{P^l_M}{P^0_M}=\frac{P^l_{LM}}{P^0_{LM}}=:\rho_j.$$ For every fixed $\epsilon_2>0$, the initial smallness choices can be made so that $|\log\rho_j|\le\epsilon_2j$ for all sufficiently large $j$.*



3.  *There are a real scalar $\sigma_Q$ and residual insertion activities $I_{h,Q}$ such that integration of the entry insertion over all shells below $v$ gives the exact field identity $$\label{eq:mag-insertion-identity}
     \mathbb E_{i:h}\mathscr I_i(K^l_{i,Q},I_{i,Q};\phi)
     =P^l_Q\left\{
        \sigma_Q\mathscr Z_h(K^l_{h,Q};\phi)
           +\mathscr I_h(K^l_{h,Q},I_{h,Q};\phi)\right\}.$$ Here $\mathbb E_{i:h}$ denotes successive shell integration, with the remaining field $\phi$ held fixed. The scalar is common to the two squares, $$\label{eq:mag-common-insertion-scalar}
     \sigma_M=\sigma_{LM}=: \sigma_j,$$ and every support of $I_{h,Q}$ contains the designated mark block. Moreover, $$\label{eq:mag-insertion-bound}
     \lVert I_{h,Q}\rVert\le C_Rs^{C_R}L^{-2(h-i)/3},
     \qquad Q\in\{M,LM\}.$$*

*The products in <a href="#eq:mag-background-products" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:mag-background-products">[eq:mag-background-products]</a> contain only the RG extraction factors. They do not include the common entry scalar or the harmonic-energy factor of the Gaussian sector law.

For $n_j=L^j/2$, there exists $\epsilon>0$ such that $$\log a_{n_{j+1}}-\log a_{n_j}
   =-\frac{\log L}{8}+\frac1{16j}
       +O_L(j^{-1-\epsilon}).
 \label{eq:terminal-ratio}$$
